Z||000013||spurdon||07-25-2001||01:04 AM||||Cliff Pennock,<P>Thank your for bringing this to our attention.  The big bandwidth crunch is supposed to occur just once when we first build our indexes for each site.  The refresh cycle that occurs once per day/week/month is typically determined on a site by site basis on their bandwidth constraints.  We are still in the beta phase testing to see what the optimum refresh cycle is.  I apologize for causing you any inconvience.  We did extensive testing to make sure that we did not disrupt normal site operations.<P>Boardreader.com is designed to serve the message board user and the webmaster(s) of smaller sites such as yours.  Our members feel that by collaborating they can share users and thus increase traffic.  This was a project that was co-developed with webmasters such as yourself.  It's also a great way for other enthusiasts to find your message board and join your community.<P>As far as the email issue, it was my impression that any emails found as part of our day to day operations are those visible to the general public in the users profile which is an OPTIONAL field in which to fill out.  Boardreader's policy is that we DO NOT share or distribute any email gathered in our normal operations to any other company or organization.  We may use this information to market ourselves and our services to like minded enthusiasts with an easy way to opt out.  If you are getting unwanted emails, it is NOT from us.<P>We can make the change and remove you and your site from our search results if that is your wish.  It will take a few weeks for everything to propogate through our system.  I do wish that you would reconsider and that we work out a way that we can include your site in our community.  The 'Net is built of so many fragmented communities it would be wonderful if we could just share in the joys of being automotive enthusiasts.<P>Feel free to respond here or to email me directly at psmith@boardreader.com or at (734) 480-0116<P>Sincerely,<P>Peter Smith<BR>co-founder<BR>Boardreader.com<P>||||reg||1
Z||000014||SteveJ||07-25-2001||09:22 AM||||I've been over to Boardreader and I like it.||||reg||1
Z||000015||Cliff Pennock||07-26-2001||08:02 AM||||   <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by spurdon:<BR><B>Thank your for bringing this to our attention.  The big bandwidth crunch is supposed to occur just once when we first build our indexes for each site.  The refresh cycle that occurs once per day/week/month is typically determined on a site by site basis on their bandwidth constraints.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>And how do you determine a site's bandwidth constraint? By saturating a site's bandwidth through downloading every message you can find?<P>   <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR><B>As far as the email issue, it was my impression that any emails found as part of our day to day operations are those visible to the general public in the users profile which is an OPTIONAL field in which to fill out.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>No, this field is <u>not</u> optional and your software is very specifically harvesting these email addresses. And judging by the responses in this thread so far, my guess is that most people here do not appreciate this fact.<P>   <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>[<B>Boardreader's policy is that we DO NOT share or distribute any email gathered in our normal operations to any other company or organization.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>And what would be <u>abnormal</u> operation?<P>   <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR><B>We may use this information to market ourselves and our services to like minded enthusiasts with an easy way to opt out.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Which is, no matter how you slice it, <B>spam</B>.<P>   <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR><B>If you are getting unwanted emails, it is NOT from us.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Wait. First you tell me you will/may use these email addresses for marketing purposes (i.e. send us unwanted email) and now you tell me that if we <B>do</B> receive unwanted email, it's not from you. What makes you think I <B>want</B> to receive email from you for marketing purposes?<P>   <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR><B>We can make the change and remove you and your site from our search results if that is your wish.  It will take a few weeks for everything to propogate through our system.  I do wish that you would reconsider and that we work out a way that we can include your site in our community.  The 'Net is built of so many fragmented communities it would be wonderful if we could just share in the joys of being automotive enthusiasts.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Oh I agree it's a wonderful idea. In fact, I wish I had thought of it myself. It has tremendous commercial potential.<P>But your site is doing stuff that will not be appreciated by <B>a lot</B> of message board owners. First, you are <B>copying</B> the complete contents to your own server. Personally, I don't like that one bit. Normally a webmaster <B>chooses</B> to submit his site to a search engine. You speak of serving webmasters of smaller sites. It is in fact these very same webmasters that will not appreciate the extra bandwidth for which they usually have to pay. And you never gave them a choice.<P>Second, and that's what's bothering me the most, you are harvesting email addresses and hide behind the fact that in your opinion, this is optional, public information. Well, it is not. You continue to state that I will never receive unwanted email from you. I can only translate that to "I will never receive email from you". If that's true, why would you need my email address in the first place?<BR><p>[This message has been edited by Cliff Pennock (edited 07-26-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000016||jlruiz67||07-26-2001||08:59 AM||||Hello Cliff and all forum members.<P>   It’s a shame that we are going this route on this post. I submit to you that this is a public forum and that the information posted is public. I hope that we all felt the same way a few weeks back when this forum died for a day or just a few hours. The idea that I can reach this forum's valuable information from another server, keeps me hopeful that if Cliff has a health problem or something happens that the forum has to close again, we will still be able to find all the great information in another place. It will lower your bandwidth consumption in the end because people would get the posts from another server. If we don't want to get spamed we should not provide our email address on a public forum. I have no personal gain from any of this. Also this will increase the exposure for our forum and we would probably get more members in the end. I hope no one takes this the wrong way, I just think that this could be a great thing for us, and maybe we should give it a little more time Cliff.<P><BR>Thank you very much for this Forum Cliff, <P>jlruiz67<BR>Jorge Ruiz<BR>||||reg||9
Z||000017||mbramble||07-26-2001||09:45 AM||||I'd say it's more of a private forum with a very liberal attitude as to who is allowed to post and what they can post.  What I mean is, there are no rights to post here, no rights to recourse if your topic/post is removed or edited or closed, no rights to free speech, etc.  Cliff could shut the whole thing down this afternoon and there's not a thing anyone could do about it.  He could remove every post you or I or anyone ever made and there is nothing that can be done about it.  He could disable your username just because he didn't like it and your only recourse would be to create another one.  And by tracking your IP address he could prevent you from doing even that.  How "public" is that??  <P>Don't get me wrong, I have absolutely NO problem with all that; and Cliff does a great job at letting the board run itself without jumping in and excercising the ultimate control he has over the whole thing.  Just trying to point out that Cliff is the master of this domain and should he not want folks in copying the entire thing, increasing bandwidth that he pays for,  and downloading members' e-mail addresses (which I completely agree with also), then it is his call.<P>As far as the information on the Forum being available in other places, a good chunk of it is available on CD from Cliff.  And, even with it 'available' on another server, did you notice where you ended up after clicking one of the thread links at boardreader.com??  Right back here at fiero.nl.  So, if the Cliff's server was down, would you simply get an error when clicking on any of the links to the Forum from boardreader.com??  If it's simply one more index, what's the benefit??  Greater exposure to the Forum??  I think that Cliff (or any other webmaster) should determine the exposure that their site gets.  ||||reg||1
Z||000018||spurdon||07-26-2001||12:53 PM||||Cliff and all forum members,<P>Thank you all for taking the time to respond and add your thoughts and comments to this thread.  I appreciate everyone's views and opinions.  fiero.nl will be removed from boardreader.com, I will make this change today.  Expect your site to disappear from our search results in about a week.<P>I would like to touch on a couple of points.  The first is the ability to be included in the BoardReader search results.  While I would love to give every site the option to be included, at the beginning of a project such as this, it is simply not feasible to contact each and every site personally and determine if they want to be included.  While this might sound rude, it is simply the realities of the business.  Does everyone think that when google, nothern light or other search engines that first started out asked each site to be included.  While I would love to have a policy such as this (selective opt in), it will simply not be feasible until a little further down the road.  I am trying to keep as close to the source as possible by reading posts, and making it as easy as possible with phone, email, etc for people to contact us with their questions and concerns.<P>Secondly, on this email issue, I realize that this is a touchy subject with a lot of people.  I will investigate into what emails that we are retrieving and delete all addresses that may have been retained from fiero.nl  We have an initial letter already in the works, so if any of you get any promotional stuff from us I apologize.  The email marketing came about from the fact that we saw that some people had listed their email in their profile. We thought that it would be okay to send these people an email when we opened since they were already using forums and we had what I consider to be a service they might be interested in.  In fact for a while, we were cutting and pasting any addresses into excel before we combined this into our other scripts.  I simply used a judgement call on this.  I use a lot of message boards (specifically about grand nationals and camaros) and I tried to put myself in the shoes of a user.  Would it be alright to send a fellow forum user an email telling them of our service.  I thought so.  <P>Most of the unwanted emails that people receive are from large companies that sell their registration lists to marketeers that then use these addresses to send you unrelated advertisements, anything from credit cards, free giveaways, etc, which in my eyes is quite different than the select approach we will be using.  What I meant by unwanted emails is that we will never share any emails with any other company whatsoever.  So any unwanted email that people get (other than a specific BoardReader one) will absolutely not be from us.<P>Cliff, I feel that with an actual product to sell (your CD) you have the most to gain from this.  Do you think that 100% of the fiero community knows about this site?  Do you feel that you would not like to grow any larger than you are now?  Boardreader has gotten a ton of site submissions in the last few days and each one of the submitted sites will be displaying a BR banner ad, effectively marketing us (and YOU) to a greater number of interested automotive enthusiasts.<P>Jlruiz67 made an excellent point in the fact that one of the things that we can offer to message boards such as yours is extended storage capacity.  Many times sites are not able to hold all the information and their servers crash, problems occur and information and community is lost.  Soon we will be able to offer sites solutions to these problems.  And by displaying and hosting old posts that are no longer available on your server, we are effectively creating more search result hits for you.<P>Cliff, you know where to find us if and when you want to join up.  I wish you the best of luck with your website and building your community.<P>Sincerely,<P>Peter Smith<BR>co-founder<BR>Boardreader.com<P>||||reg||1
Z||000019||AusFiero||07-26-2001||07:16 PM||||Well I know my email address is only in my profile so other forum members HERE ONLY can contact me.<P>As the owner of several domains I am sick of the 60 odd spam mails I receive every day from companies who just assume it is ok to harvest email addresses. I thought there was lawas against it in the USA. Anyone know that for sure? <P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.jadeweb.com.au/fiero/ausfiero1.gif"> <BR><A HREF="http://www.jadeweb.com.au/fiero" TARGET=_blank>My Fiero</A> | <A HREF="http://www.auto-alley.com" TARGET=_blank>Fiero Accessories</A>||202.139.20.3||reg||1
Z||000020||Cliff Pennock||07-26-2001||07:23 PM||||First of all, the CD is <u>not</u> a commercial product, and that's <u>not</u> what this site is about. If you think I want more traffic so I can sell more CDs, than you are dead wrong. Second, you are making the assumption that <u>all</u> message boards would appreciate more traffic. That's mistake number two. Third, you are doing something that no other search engine is doing: indexing pages that are created by scripts. That's the reason why you will not find individual threads on any search site. Forth, you <B>are</B> creating a tremendous amount of extra bandwidth that will hurt the smaller messageboard. A lot (if not most) messageboards reside on either free servers, or on their own personal homepage. By downloading their entire contents at once, they will hit their quota much, much sooner and they'll either have to shut down their site until the end of the month, or have to pay extra. Fifth, <B>you are harvesting email addresses</B>. The email addresses are visible as a service to <B>members</B>, so they can discuss certain topics in private email. Now, in order to protect my members from being spammed, I'll have to remove the public exposure of email addresses.<P>In short, you are pretending to do us (me) a favor, while I never asked for any such favor nor did my members.<P>If people are worried that the contents of this forum will disappear because something happens to me or if I decide to shut the forum down, well, start your own forum then.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000021||jake1852||07-26-2001||07:35 PM||||kick his ass, cliff<P>i dont know about anyone else, but i have a "block sender" list on my e-mail that is huge. i get no spam at all. THE PAST WEEK OR SO I HAVE GOTTEN TONS!<BR>coincidence? i dunno||||reg||1
Z||000022||spurdon||07-27-2001||12:22 AM||||I'm not going to try and tell people the way to run their site nor am I going to tell people about what is happening on the internet with mass emails.  If you think boardreader is the first or the last site to deep dive a site for cgi script or deep dive a site for emails then you all are deadly wrong.  Lets face it in order for smaller sites to live on the internet they all need some type of revenue to pay for their space on the internet.  It is Cliffs problem how he pays for his bandwidth and he doesn't want boardreader.com a part of his site using what he feels is a waste of bandwidth.  In Cliffs eyes this use of bandwidth which in reality is not that much under refresh conditions is not worth a dime for the possiblity of bring new and interesting people/conversation to his board.  This is a choice of fiero.nl and no one can debate this.  That is fine.  No hard feelings let me say that.  I don't believe in many of Cliffs comments and that is fine as well but I am not here begging to list his site.  If people/webmasters want to be involved in what we think is a neat and helpful tool on the internet than great.  If not than well fine.  The point is that we are not out to cause these huge problems like people are saying.  The internet is about choice and this is what has happened over the past few days on <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl." TARGET=_blank>www.fiero.nl.</A>   It is choice if Cliff wants to be a part of boardreader.  It is choice if Cliff wants to have a message board about Fiero's.  With this said it has always been these choices that make life interesting.  I am not going to lie to you all.  This feeling at this site has not been fun.  It has been interesting because I have never heard such negitive reaction towards what boardreader is trying to do.  I don't feel like I was well received therefore my time here is over.  I'm sure some of you will be happy.  I know Cliff will feel like he has one some battle that we at boardreader were never trying to fight.  Have fun all of you.  We will miss out on these great conversations but I think we all will be happier on our own way.  Good luck to all at this board and I respect all your feelings.  Please don't take this note as me being frustrated or pissed. I'm not either of these things I just hope you all who use this board and that Cliff can look back on this conversation and be happy with the outcome.  Thank you all for your time.  Good bye!<BR>P.S.  For those of you who think we have sent emails out or that believe we have sold emails to others you are all WRONG!!  Not one single email has been sent by boardreader or by anyone related to boardreader.com.  <p>[This message has been edited by spurdon (edited 07-27-2001).]||||reg||12
Z||000023||Archie||07-27-2001||08:08 AM||||GOODBYE, Spam-man.<P>Archie||||reg||14
Z||000024||Cliff Pennock||07-27-2001||08:14 AM||||"Won a battle"??? "Use of bandwidth really not that much"???<P>Are you for real? Listen bud, this hasn't been a battle at all but for some reason you choose to avoid the concerns I'm raising.<P>Let me state a few facts here.<P>You download the complete contents of this forum which I believe is a total waste of bandwidth. For your information, in only <B>two weeks</B> your site has downloaded <B>853Mb</B> of data. I'm not sure why you call that "not much". Then I find out your site is harvesting email addresses. Then you register to this forum with the <B>sole purpose to promote your own site</B>. Your exact words: "Check out this message board for car enthusiast". Your site <B>isn't</B> for car enthousiast. Unless you wish to explain to me what "Arts & Antiques", "Boating" and "Console Games" have to do with cars. Any way you slice it, you are a <B>spammer</B>, Spurdon. And I don't appreciate that. You could have just send me an email telling me about your site and if I would be interested in telling my members about your site. But you chose to ignore the one big rule most messageboards have: no spamming!<P>Then you say something like: "it is simply not feasible to contact each and every site personally and determine if they want to be included". I don't get it Spurdon. It's not feasible to contact the <B>owner's</B> of the messageboards, but you do take the time to <B>spam</B> them personally? And you have all these wonderful scripts that leeches all messageboards? Why not write a script that will contact the administrators?<P>Face it Spurdon, your site is doing stuff it shouldn't and all your efforts to make <B>me</B> look bad will not change that.<P>It also p!sses me off that you pretend that this forum (or all other messageboard as well for that matter) <B>need</B> you. Well, wake up call: we did fine without you and we will continue to do fine without you. Fiero owners will sooner find <B>my</B> site than they will find <B>yours</B>.<P>I wish you well with your venture, but I am pretty sure I am not the first and most certainly not the last administrator that frowns at your practices.||||reg||1
Z||000025||spurdon||07-28-2001||11:20 PM||||Cliff and all forum members,<P>I realize that our site has been the reason for great controversy over here at fiero.nl, and has received both good and bad responses from different members of this forum.  It was not our intention to cause any of these things.  Cliff has had two problems with our relationship which are 1) emails 2) use of bandwidth.  I have investigated these issues further and I want to post the scoop for all of the fiero.nl users.<P>1. We do not "specifically" harvest or retrieve any emails from this board or any other boards. Cliff made it sound that we have specific email software that goes in and automatically takes everyone's emails and compiles lists.  This is not true. We do get some emails in the posts that we index, however no action has been taken on these addresses and I am working hard to delete any from fiero.nl  Cliff asked why do we take the emails if we don't intend to use them.  The answer is that it is technically easier to index the whole post rather that break it up and we might use them to advertise for our service in the future.<P>2.  Cliff does have a legitimate problem with bandwidth use.  We did testing to minimize the bandwidth usage on other sites, but Cliff did have a problem.  Here's why:  Fiero.nl has an archive section which our software somehow looped into.  Since Cliff has a large archive section, he experienced abnormally high bandwidth from our site.  Again, this was not our intention to cause a large bandwidth draw on individual sites, and for this rare problem, I apologize.<P>So here is what BoardReader.com is going to do.  If Cliff will contact me personally at psmith@boardreader.com I will PAY him $100.00 towards bandwidth reimbursement for his hosting bill.  I am doing this to address a problem that we created and are willing to fix.  I want Cliff and the fiero.nl members to know that we are not in the business of creating problems for sites such as Cliff's.<P>Sincerely,<P>Peter Smith<BR>co-founder<BR>Boardreader.com||||reg||1
Z||000026||Galen||07-29-2001||07:19 AM||||Why can't Peter Smith address Cliff directly instead of in the third person? If Peter Smith would do this, he would probably look less 'spammish' and make it seem as though Peter Smith is not trying to cover up his activities. <P>Sincerely,<P>Ray<BR>co-founder<BR>Whut You Be Saying Bout Me, Inc.<P>------------------<BR><FONT COLOR="purple">Ray</FONT> <FONT COLOR="gray">-</FONT> <FONT COLOR="purple">"And God said there shall be one in thy owns image, and he made me"</FONT><BR><A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/ray19nc/download.html" TARGET=_blank>Download FieroProject.com Chat Client v1.50 Today</A>||||reg||1
Z||000027||Cliff Pennock||07-29-2001||07:27 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR><FONT COLOR="red">Originally posted by spurdon:</FONT><BR>1. We do not "specifically" harvest or retrieve any emails from this board or any other boards. Cliff made it sound that we have specific email software that goes in and automatically takes everyone's emails and compiles lists.  This is not true.<HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Yes it is. I have the log files to back this up. My log files show your site is specifically going for the email addresses seperate from the messages.<P> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>So here is what BoardReader.com is going to do.  If Cliff will contact me personally at psmith@boardreader.com I will PAY him $100.00 towards bandwidth reimbursement for his hosting bill.<HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>That's very generous of you, but I am not concerned about any extra costs this is causing <B>me</B>. I happen to have my own servers and dedicated 2Mbit line. My worry was about the thousands of small messageboards out there that <B>do</B> have to pay for the extra bandwidth. Are you going to give <u>them</u> $100?<P>Peter, don't get me wrong. I think boardreader.com is a wonderful idea and I'm sure it's going to be successful. I just think you are handling it the wrong way. When I started this forum, I politely asked a lot of Fiero related sites for a link. I posted a message in a handful of related newsgroups, and I did not fill my database with messages from other sites. And right now, this forum is the largest Fiero community on the internet.<P>In short, you <B>earn</B> success, you don't try to force it.||||reg||1
Z||000028||hugh||07-29-2001||08:18 AM||||Thanks Cliff,for looking out for your friends.||||reg||1
